WRITTEN=[
('speech1',17,'Two sound pairs differ by the same acoustic distance. Only one pair crosses a labeling boundary. Predict their discrimination under a linear noncategorical account and under categorical perception. Explain why collecting labeling responses alone is insufficient.', ['A linear equal-sensitivity account predicts similar discrimination for matched physical distances.','Categorical perception predicts better discrimination across the boundary.','Forced category labels can themselves look categorical; linking discrimination peaks to boundaries provides the additional evidence.']),
('speech1',32,'Compare the effects of nonspeech categorization, chinchilla speech categorization, and experience-dependent improvement on a difficult infant nasal contrast. Identify the distinct initial claim each finding challenges.', ['Nonspeech categories challenge restriction to linguistic stimuli.','Chinchilla results challenge restriction to humans with language.','Improvement with input challenges a universal starting state in which learning merely deletes unused contrasts.']),
('speech2',12,'Both tokens are ultimately clicked as bear, but the near-boundary token attracts more looks to pear. Explain the predictions of a completed-phoneme gate and a cascaded account. Why does the categorical final response not decide between them?', ['A gate transmitting one identical completed symbol loses the within-category variation.','A cascade passes graded uncertainty forward, increasing competitor activation near the boundary.','The endpoint records the selected category; the eye trace records competition before selection.']),
('speech2',23,'Explain how Ganong’s lexical boundary effect and the plug/blood/blush simulation relate. Distinguish cascading from feedback and describe what the final /g/ contributes.', ['Cascading transmits partial evidence continuously forward.','Final /g/ favors plug and weakens candidates with incompatible endings.','Feedback from an activated lexical candidate reinforces compatible phonemes; the mechanism links word knowledge to ambiguous phoneme identity.']),
('sentence',14,'The instruction stays put the apple on the towel in the box. Compare a one-apple and two-apple scene. Predict looks to the empty towel and explain why the contrast matters for syntax-first versus interactive accounts.', ['One apple makes on the towel less necessary for reference and permits a destination interpretation.','Two apples make the phrase useful for identifying which apple, reducing looks to the irrelevant destination.','The same words produce different early competition as referential context changes, supporting early interaction.']),
('sentence',29,'Explain why an early category-violation response need not establish an autonomous grammar-only mechanism. Use form typicality, perches/needs, and neutral nouns to develop the alternative.', ['The context predicts a category and can also predict associated physical word forms.','Perches and needs separate typical form associations from the category used in a given context.','The dependence on morphological or statistical form cues, with no equivalent neutral-noun effect, links the early response partly to perceptual prediction.']),
('aphasias',14,'Why compare ordinary active sentences with reversible and improbable embedded sentences in aphasia? Explain the original grammatical interpretation and how healthy listeners under stress change the debate.', ['Ordinary plausibility and first-noun order can support an answer without resolving every structural relation.','Reversible and improbable items make grammatical role assignment more diagnostic.','A similar structural vulnerability under speed or memory load supports a performance-resource contribution, rather than a grammar-only conclusion.']),
('aphasias',21,'Design a comparison using verbal and nonverbal stimuli to test a language-specific account of comprehension difficulty. Explain the relevant prediction using the Saygin studies.', ['Preserve an action relation or auditory meaning-matching demand while changing linguistic status.','A language-exclusive deficit predicts disproportionate linguistic difficulty under suitably matched demands.','Difficulty in both action or sound formats, with overlapping lesion associations, supports broader shared contributions.']),
('development1',17,'In Maye’s experiment, the groups hear tokens 3 and 6 equally often. Explain what differs in familiarization, how testing works, and why the result supports distributional learning.', ['The overall distribution is unimodal or bimodal, favoring one or two clusters.','Test trials alternate the critical tokens or repeat one, measuring a behavioral difference between conditions.','Only the bimodal group shows clear sensitivity; equal critical-token frequency limits a simple familiarity explanation.']),
('development1',18,'Compare live Mandarin exposure with recorded exposure, and contingent with noncontingent social responses. Explain what the infant experiences in each comparison and the proposed learning mechanisms.', ['Live exchange supplies an interactive speaker rather than only auditory or audiovisual input.','Contingent social responses follow the infant’s own vocal behavior; noncontingent responses do not.','Social interaction can reinforce behavior or guide learning toward relevant information, rather than acting merely as additional sound exposure.']),
('development2',9,'A trained artificial word and a part-word each occur twice. Explain how their transitional probabilities can still differ, and interpret longer looking to the part-word after exposure.', ['The denominator includes all occurrences of the starting syllable, not just the complete test sequence.','An internal continuation can be reliable while an equally frequent boundary sequence follows only some starting syllables.','A novelty preference for matched-frequency part-words supports learning conditional sequence structure beyond simple count familiarity.']),
('development2',24,'Contrast the evidence from a short utterance such as mommy up with comprehension of the duck is gorping the bunny. Explain why a novel verb and reversed-role displays improve the inference.', ['A short utterance can be a formula or a productive combination; its surface form alone does not distinguish them.','A novel verb reduces reliance on a stored verb-specific phrase.','Choosing the correct actor–recipient relation demonstrates generalization of transitive word order despite limited production.']),
('l1-critical',18,'Compare later ASL acquisition in a person with an early spoken L1 and in a person without accessible early language. Explain what similar ASL-onset ages do and do not equate.', ['Similar ASL onset holds the age of learning that particular language closer.','Prior accessible language remains different: one is late L2, the other late L1.','The stronger late-L2 outcome supports an early-language foundation, rather than treating all late sign learning as equivalent.']),
('l1-critical',6,'Explain the amblyopia analogy for a sensitive period. Include early competition, patching, and adult intensive training; distinguish an early advantage from a complete loss of later plasticity.', ['Abnormal early input gives one eye a cortical competitive advantage that can outlast the peripheral problem.','Patching the stronger eye changes that balance.','Adult improvement after suitable training permits later plasticity while retaining the importance of an early optimal window.']),
('l2-learning',13,'Explain why receptive cognate success alone could overstate learning in a paired-associate experiment. Use productive testing and the week-later results to strengthen the interpretation.', ['A transparent form can make a familiar meaning guessable without a learned retrieval route.','Productive testing requires generating the new form from its known associate.','A cognate benefit in production and delayed retention supports both acquisition and persistence of mappings.']),
('l2-learning',20,'Describe the English-target/Russian-competitor visual-world result and the reverse-language comparison. Use the Revised Hierarchical Model to explain the asymmetry, then predict why balanced bilinguals need not behave as isolated monolinguals.', ['Russian-name competitors attract looks during English processing; the reverse effect is not reliable in the reported Russian task.','Asymmetric lexical and conceptual links give L1 stronger influence on L2 in this learner configuration.','Strong links in both languages can sustain competition and control demands rather than separating the systems completely.']),
('l2-sensitive',9,'State the exercise and maturational-state hypotheses in words. Explain which is more directly compatible with lower late-L2 attainment among people who acquired an early L1.', ['The exercise hypothesis preserves capacity through early successful use.','The maturational-state hypothesis allows capacity to decline despite that use.','Late-L2 difficulty after normal early L1 is more directly compatible with maturation than complete preservation by exercise.']),
('l2-sensitive',15,'An untimed grammaticality test shows an age-of-arrival relationship. Explain why its interpretation should consider schooling, construction type, language pairing, and experience history rather than naming one cause from the scatterplot.', ['Untimed judgments can draw on explicitly taught metalinguistic rules.','Structures and language pairings differ in available transfer and difficulty.','Age covaries with exposure histories, so the association alone does not isolate one maturational mechanism.'])]
